<p>The article's been rewritten since you posted this (I guess you were composing this while I posted the new version).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As already stated, this is a Safe-class SCP.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I realized I'd misinterpreted the article on Object Classes, yes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So bacteria are a problem with this thing. That makes sense, but you don't tell us to keep this thing itself sterile. No decontamination of the device prior to or following use. Just that we test it in a &quot;specially constructed testing environment.&quot; It would help to state it's a clean room, everything going in and coming out has to be decontaminated, etc., rather than implying it by telling people not to let any foreign organisms in, and to clarify that the device itself must be kept sterile.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Dumb as it might sound, I had about six different versions of what that special testing environment was, but none of them seem to make a lot of sense. Because none of them mentioned it being sterile. *headdesk*</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The &quot;BURN IT ALL&quot; contingency is not bad in and of itself, but with the minimal attention that's given to telling people how to avoid it, it seems a wonder this thing isn't a pile of slag by now.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>True.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We test this on people because we apparently just wanna watch them wither away, instead of lab rats or guinea pigs or what have you, and ignore the fact that people are distinctly not sterile. Nor is most any living thing, but a rat bred and grown in a controlled environment would be distinctly more so than a person. Also easier to replace.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Actually, a remote drone would be easier. I guess my point didn't come across well enough. I meant to convey that SHOULD D-Class personnel be used for testing, they should not be told about 281's effects. This to make sure they didn't get any fancy ideas about what to do with a handy little time stopping device. Even if it aged them 63 years compared to the rest of us. I agree with you that I didn't phrase that too well (what a euphemism.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is a pretty lame redaction and doesn't add anything. This is a cop out to blackbox how the SCP functions. I don't mean paste ████ over it, though it does that. I mean that it clumsily obfuscates how it functions, which is pointless, since you go on to say that despite our belief for some unknown reason this component is the source of the timefuck, we still can't replicate the effect despite presumably being able to duplicate it in full.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Point taken. It was meant to signify the danger of someone replicating its effects. Which I contradicted later on, as you point out.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Lastly, the addendum feels like it's trying far too hard to inflate this thing's importance.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I suppose I wanted to make it more badass than &quot;press snooze and have 9 minutes to yourself&quot;, though to be honest the idea was that the guy they found in that mountain cabin was a total conspiracy nut who got lucky. I'll edit it a bit more to show that.</p>

